Using these two maps one has the fundamental formula
\cite[Prop II.1.9]{nikolaus-scholze} 
\begin{equation} \mathrm{TC}(X) = \mathrm{fib}( \mathrm{TC}^-(X)
\xrightarrow{\mathrm{can}_X - \varphi_X} \mathrm{TP}(X)),\end{equation}
or equivalently $\mathrm{TC}(X)$ is the equalizer of $\mathrm{can}_X$,
$\varphi_X$.

Namely, given a nonunital $R$-algebra $I$, we can build a local nonunital $R$-algebra $I[(1+I)^{-1}]$ as follows: the elements formally written $``1+x"$ for $x\in I$ form a commutative monoid under multiplication, and this monoid acts on $I$ by multiplication.  This makes $I$ into a non-unital algebra over the monoid ring $R[1+I]$, and we can set
\begin{equation} I[(1+I)^{-1}] := I\otimes_{R[1+I]}
R[(1+I)^{gp}], \end{equation}
where $(1+I)^{gp}$ is the group completion.

Let $R$ be an ind-smooth $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra. Then, for each $n \geq 0$, one has:  egin{enumerate} \item (Geisser--Levine \cite{GL}) There is a natural map $\pi_n ( K(R)/p) ightarrow 
u^n(R)$, which is an isomorphism if $R$ is local. \item (Geisser--Hesselholt \cite{GH}) There is a functorial exact sequence $0 	o \widetilde{
u}^{n+1}(R) 	o \pi_n ( \mathrm{TC}(R)/p) 	o {
u}^{n}(R) 	o 0$. Furthermore, under these identifications, the composite of $\pi_n ( K(R)/p) 	o \pi_n (\mathrm{TC}(R)/p) 	o 
u^n(R) $ (where the first map arises from the cyclotomic trace) is the map of (1). \item If $R$ is local, we have a functorial identification $\pi_n ( K^{\mathrm{inv}}(R)/p) \simeq \widetilde{
u}^{n+2}(R)$. \end{enumerate}

Let $(R, I)$ be a henselian pair of $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebras. Then for each $n$, the map $
u^n(R) 	o 
u^n(R/I)$ is surjective and the map $\widetilde{
u}^{n}(R) 	o \widetilde{
u}^n(R/I)$ is an isomorphism.

We use the commutative diagram \[ \xymatrix{ V_0 \ar[d] \ar[r] & V_1 \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_R \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} \ar[d] & \Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_{R/I} \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} & \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I} }\] where we define $V_0, V_1$ to be the kernels of the surjective vertical maps $\Omega^n_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}$ and $\Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I}$. We will show that the map $V_0 	o V_1$ is surjective. This easily implies the desired conclusions about the maps $
u^n(R) 	o 
u^{n}(R/I)$ and $\widetilde{
u}^n(R) 	o \widetilde{
u}^n(R/I)$ thanks to the snake lemma. Consider a differential form $\omega = a dx_1 dx_2 \dots dx_n \in \Omega^n_R$ such that one of $\left\{a, x_1, \dots, x_night\}$ belongs to $I$ (here $n = 0$ is allowed). The image of such a class in $\Omega^n_R/d \Omega_R^{n-1}$ belongs to $V_1$, and $V_1$ is generated by such classes. For $u \in R$, we have $$(1 - C^{-1}) ( u \omega) = (u - u^p a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1}) \omega.$$ Since $(R, I)$ is a henselian pair and $a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1}\in I$, we can choose $u \in R$ such that $u - u^p a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1} = 1$, using Lemma~ef{solveeq:hens}. The class $u \omega \in \Omega^n_R$ belongs to $V_0$ and has image given by $\omega$; so $\omega$ is in the image of $V_0 	o V_1$, as desired.

Let $R$ be any local $\mathbb F_p$-algebra, $n\ge-1$, and $r\ge 1$. Then the trace map $\pi_n (K(R)/p^r) 	o \pi_n (\mathrm{TC}(R)/p^r)$ is split injective. More precisely, there is a functor $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}:\operatorname{CAlg}_{\mathbb{F}_p}ightarrow\operatorname{Ab}$ and a natural transformation $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(-)ightarrow \pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(-)/p^r)$ such that, for $R$ local, the induced map $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus \pi_n(K(R)/p^r)\longrightarrow\pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(R)/p^r)$$ is an isomorphism. This is also compatible with the natural transition maps as $r$ varies. Moreover, $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}$ commutes with filtered colimits; in particular, the direct sum decomposition above holds for arbitrary $R$ after Zariski sheafification.

Recall that if $R$ is an $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra, we can make a functorial surjection to $R$ from a polynomial algebra, namely take the polynomial algebra $\mathbb{F}_p[x_a]_{a\in R}$ on variables indexed by the elements of $R$ with the map $x_a\mapsto a$. Let $R'ightarrow R$ denote the henselization of this surjection at its kernel; thus $R'$ is a functorial ind-smooth $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra surjecting onto $R$ with henselian kernel. Moreover, $R\mapsto R'$ evidently commutes with filtered colimits. Define for $m \geq 0$ $$\widetilde{
u^m_r}(R) := H^1(\operatorname{Spec}(R')_\sub{'{e}t};\pi_{m}(\mathcal{TC}/p^r)).$$ Since the coefficient presheaf $\pi_{m}(\mathrm{TC}(-)/p^r)$ commutes with filtered colimits by Corollary ef{TCring}, this 'etale cohomology group commutes with filtered colimits in $R'$ by standard cocontinuity arguments. Combining with the previous, we find that $\widetilde{
u^m_r}(-)$ commutes with filtered colimits. Furthermore, since $\operatorname{Spec}(R')$ has '{e}tale $p$-cohomological dimension $\leq 1$, the descent spectral sequence for $\mathrm{TC}(-)/p^r$ gives a natural map $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)ightarrow \pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(R')/p^r)$ (compare with the proofs of Theorem ef{GHGL} and Theorem ef{padicetaleKthy}). Composing with the map on $\mathrm{TC}$ induced by $R'ightarrow R$ defines the desired natural transformation $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)ightarrow \pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(R)/p^r)$. Now assume $R$ is local. Then $R'$ is too, since a henselian ideal is radical, and it is moreover ind-smooth. Thus the argument recalled before the statement of the theorem, based on the results of Geisser--Levine and Geisser--Hesselholt, shows that $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus \pi_n(K(R')/p^r)\overset{\sim}{ightarrow}\pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(R')/p^r).$$ On the other hand our main rigidity theorem, Theorem ef{mainthm}, gives a long exact sequence $$\ldotsightarrow\pi_n(K(R')/p^r)ightarrow \pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(R')/p^r)\oplus \pi_n(K(R)/p^r)ightarrow \pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(R)/p^r)ightarrow\ldots.$$ Combining shows both that this long exact sequence breaks up into short exact sequences and that $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus\pi_n(K(R)/p^r)\overset{\sim}{ightarrow}\pi_n(\mathrm{TC}(R)/p^r),$$ as claimed.

When $r=1$, the proof of Theorem ef{GHGL} shows that
$\widetilde{
u^m_1}(R)= \widetilde{
u^m}(R')$, and this combines with Proposition ef{toyFprigidityTC} to give an identification
$$\widetilde{
u^m_1}(R)= \widetilde{
u^m}(R):=\operatorname{coker}( 1 - C^{-1}: \Omega^m_R 	o \Omega^m_R / d \Omega^{m-1}_R)$$
for arbitrary $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebras $R$ and $m \geq 0$.

As in \cite[Sec.~4]{Morrow-HW}, 
we have a natural map 
\[ \overline{F}: W_r \Omega_R^{m} \to W_r \Omega_R^{m}/ d V^{r-1}
\Omega_R^{m}  \]
for an arbitrary $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra $R$ which factors the Frobenius $F: W_{r+1}
\Omega_R^{m} \to W_r \Omega_R^{m}$.

Let $\pi: W_r \Omega_R^{m} \to 
 W_r \Omega_R^{m}/ d V^{r-1} \Omega_R^{m}$ be the natural projection and 
consider the map 
 \begin{equation} W _r \Omega_R^{m} \xrightarrow{\pi - \overline{F}}  
 W_r \Omega_R^{m}/ (d V^{r-1} \Omega_R^{m})
.\end{equation}

There is a commutative diagram
of spectra
\[ \xymatrix{
\mathrm{TC}^-(H \mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}})  \ar[r]^-{\varphi_{H
\mathbb{F}_p}}  \ar[d]&  
\mathrm{TP}(H \mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}}) \ar[d]  \\
H\mathbb{F}_p \ar[r]_{\varphi_{H \mathbb F_p}} &  H \mathbb{F}_p^{tC_p}
}\]
where the top row is obtained by taking $S^1$-homotopy fixed points on the bottom
row via the Tate orbit lemma, \cite[Lem.~II.4.2]{nikolaus-scholze}.

Assertion (3) follows from assertion (1) and  from the cofiber sequence $X^{hS^1}
\stackrel{\mathrm{can}_X}{\to} X^{tS^1} \to \Sigma^2 X_{hS^1}$, where we use 
that multiplication by $x$ is an equivalence on $X^{tS^1}$.

We obtain a resulting functorial commutative diagram,
where the base-changes are along $\varphi_{ H \mathbb F_p}:
\mathrm{TC}^-(H \mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}}) \to
\mathrm{TP}( H \mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}})$,
\begin{equation} \xymatrix@C=1cm{
\Sigma^{-2} \mathrm{TC}^-(X) \ar[r]^x \ar[d] &  \mathrm{TC}^-(X) \ar[d]
\ar[rd]^{\varphi_X} \\
\Sigma^{-2} \mathrm{TC}^-(X) \otimes_{\mathrm{TC}^-(H \mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}})}
\mathrm{TP}(H \mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}})
\ar[r]_-{\varphi_{H \mathbb F_p}(x)} & \mathrm{TC}^-(X)
\otimes_{\mathrm{TC}^-(H\mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}})}
\mathrm{TP}(H \mathbb{F}_p^{\mathrm{triv}}) \ar[r] & 
 \mathrm{TP}(X)  
} .\end{equation}

It now follows that there is a functorial commutative diagram
\begin{equation} \xymatrix{
\Sigma^{-2} \mathrm{TC}^-(X) \ar[d]_{x} \ar[r]^{\mathrm{id}} &\Sigma^{-2}
\mathrm{TC}^-(X)\ar[d]^{x \mathrm{can}_X} \\
\mathrm{TC}^-(X) \ar[r]_{\mathrm{can}_X-\varphi_X} &  \mathrm{TP}(X)  
} \end{equation} 
in which the fiber of the bottom arrow is $\mathrm{TC}(X)$ by formula \eqref{keyformula}.

Consider an $X \in \mathrm{CycSp}_{\geq 0}$ which is $p$-complete.  Since $\mathrm{TC}(X\otimes (-)^{\mathrm{triv}})$ is an exact functor from spectra to spectra with value $\mathrm{TC}(X)$ on $S^0$, we get a natural transformation
\[ \mathrm{TC}(X) \otimes (-) \to \mathrm{TC}(X \otimes (-)^{\mathrm{triv}}),\]
uniquely characterized by being an equivalence on $S^0$, hence on any finite spectrum.\footnote{This is a standard ``assembly map".  To construct it rigorously, note that left Kan extension of the restriction of the right hand side to finite spectra is uniquely characterized by its value on $S^0$, by the universal property of spectra among presentable stable $\infty$-categories, and therefore identifies with the left hand side.}

Since both sides are bounded below mod $p$ by Lemma \ref{connectivity}, it
follows that \eqref{TCcolim}
is an equivalence after smashing with $S^0/p$.

For any ind-smooth $\mathbb F_p$-algebra $R$, consider the commutative diagram
 \begin{equation} \xymatrix{
W\Omega^{n}_R/p\ar[r]^{F-1}\ar[d] & W\Omega^{n}_R/p\ar[d]\\
\Omega^n_R\ar[r]_{C^{-1}-1}&\Omega^n_R/d\Omega^{n-1}_R
}. \end{equation} 
Here $C^{-1}$ is the inverse Cartier operator. 
To complete the proof we will show that the two horizontal arrows have isomorphic kernels (resp.\ cokernels), i.e., the square is both cartesian and cocartesian.

Applying Proposition~\ref{milnsquarehens} to the Milnor square
\[ \xymatrix{
R\ltimes I \ar[d]  \ar[r] &  S \ar[d]  \\
R \ar[r] &  S/I
}\]
then reveals that the square
\[ \xymatrix{
R\ltimes I^{nuh} \ar[d]  \ar[r] &  S^h \ar[d]  \\
R \ar[r] &  S^h/I^h
}\]
is also Milnor, i.e., that $I^{nuh}\stackrel{\simeq}{\to} I^h$, as desired.

We use the commutative diagram
\[ \xymatrix{
V_0 \ar[d] \ar[r] &  V_1 \ar[d]  \\
\Omega^n_R \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} \ar[d]  &  \Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R  \ar[d]  \\
\Omega^n_{R/I} \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} &  \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I}
}\]
where we define $V_0, V_1$ to be the kernels of the surjective vertical maps
$\Omega^n_R \to 
\Omega^n_{R/I}$ and 
$\Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R  \to \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I}$.

The class $u \omega \in \Omega^n_R$ belongs to $V_0$ and has image given by
$\omega$; 
so $\omega$ is in the image of $V_0 \to V_1$, as desired.

To do this, we will detect symbols using the $\mathrm{dlog}$ maps from $K_2^M$ to absolute K\"ahler differentials $\Omega^2_{-}:=\Omega^2_{-/\mathbb F_p}$:
\[\xymatrix{
\Omega^2_{k[[t]]}\ar[r]&\varprojlim_s\Omega^2_{k[t]/(t^s)}\\
K_2^M(k[[t]])/p\ar[u]_{\operatorname{dlog}}\ar[r] &\varprojlim_s K_2^M(k[t]/(t^s))/p\ar[u]_{\operatorname{dlog}}
}\]
It remains to construct an element in the bottom right of the diagram whose image in the top right does not come from the top left.

Let $\sum_{i\ge 1}\tau_it^i$ be the expansion of $e(\mathrm{dlog}
f)\in\varprojlim_s\Omega^2_k\otimes_kk[t]/(t^s)$. Noting that
\[\mathrm{dlog}(1+t^jb)\wedge\mathrm{dlog}(1+t^jc)\equiv t^{2j}da\wedge db\text{ mod
}dt,\,t^{3j}\] with all the higher order terms given by various expressions in
$b,c$, we see that \[\tau_{2s-2}\equiv\sum_{i=1}^{w_{s}}b_i^{(s)}\wedge
c_i^{(s)} \text{ mod }{\Omega^2_{k_{s-1}}}.\] By the second paragraph of the proof we deduce that the image of $\tau_{2s-2}$ in $\Omega^2_{k/k_{s-1}}$ has weight $\ge w_s$, whence a fortiori $w(\tau_{2s-2})\ge w_s$. By choice of the sequence $0< w_1<\cdots$, it follows that $\mathrm{dlog} f$ cannot be lifted to $\Omega^2_{k[[t]]}$, which completes the proof.

Part (1) is an immediate consequence of the commutative diagram
\[\xymatrix{
\{K_n^M(R/I^s)/p^r\}_s\ar[r]\ar@/_1cm/[rr]_{\mathrm{dlog}[\cdot]}& \{K_n(R/I^s;\mathbb Z/p^r\mathbb Z)\}_s\ar[r]^{\cong}&\{W_r\Omega_{R/I^s,\sub{log}}^n\}_s
}\]
which summarises the statement of Theorem \ref{theorem_pro_GL}, since the bendy arrow is surjective.
